|> Exercise
    work=ing
    handle=_product_rule_physical_ver_

    In this exercise we consider two points
    in time $t_0$ and $t_0 + \Delta{}t$ (here
    “$\Delta{}t$”, read “delta $t$”, is a
    standard notation for a small amount of
    time):

    |> Image
        src=images/svg_ch4_RATS_timeline.svg

    We also consider quantities $A$ and $B$
    that are changing with time; $A$ and $B$
    have some value at $t_0$, and, say, grow
    to be larger at $t_0 + \Delta{}t$:

    |> Image
        src=images/svg_ch4_RATS_timeline_with_A_B.svg

    More specifically, we are interested in
    the change in the value of the product

    $$
    \Large AB
    $$

    over said course of time.

    To introduce an unsolicited metaphor,
    imagine $A$ and $B$ as

    |> Image
        src=images/svg_ch4_RATS_rats.svg

    that are crossing a hallway surveyed by
    a cat. One side of the hallway is time
    $t_0$, the other side of the hallway is
    time $t_0 + \Delta{}t$. So great is 
    their terror
    that $A$ and $B$ have decided to
    cross the hallway one at a time—first
    $A$ will go, then $B$. In so, 
    three moments of interest
    can be separated
    (“moments” that exist inside the
    metaphorical timeline of the story, not
    on the $t$-number line, to be clear):

    |> ImageLeft
        offset_x= 0.5em
        offset_y=-0.5em
        src=images/svg_ch4_RATS_corridor_cloud.svg

    |> TextParent
        |> ol
            style=margin:0 1.5em 0.5em 2em;list-style:auto;list-style-position:outside;

            |> li
                style=margin-top:0.5em
                when $A$ and $B$ are both still at $t_0$

            |> li
                style=margin-top:0.5em
                when $A$ has made it to $t_0 + \Delta{}t$,
                and $B$ is still at $t_0$

            |> li
                style=margin-top:0.5em
                |> del
                    when _B_'s tail is sticking out of the
                    cat's mouth, and
                \ when $A$ and $B$ have both made it
                to $t_0 + \Delta{}t$

    Correspondingly, the product

    $$
    \Large AB
    $$

    changes in two increments: first as $A$
    makes it to the other side of the hallway,
    and $A$ grows bigger; then as $B$ joins
    him/her, and $B$ grows bigger. In an
    equation:

    |> Image
        src=images/svg_ch4_RATS_equation.svg

    If we divide the above equation by $\Delta{}t$
    and let $\Delta{}t$ drop to $0$, what does
    each term become?

    |> Solution

        Dividing by $\Delta{}t$:

        |> Image
            src=images/svg_ch4_RATS_equation_over_Dt.svg

        The first term on the left-hand side comes
        out to

        $$
        {(AB)(t_0 + \Delta t) - (AB)(t_0)\over \Delta t}
        $$

        when written out, which approaches

        |> Boxed
            $$
            (AB)'(t_0)
            $$

        as $\Delta t$ approaches $0$, 
        because it has the
        form 

        $$
        {f(t_0 + \Delta{}t) - f(t_0)\over \Delta{}t}
        $$

        of a difference quotient, with $f = AB$. (Cf. Note
        >>_difference_quotient_limit_ of Exercise >>_difference_quotient_illustration_.)
        (Nb: We assume differentiability, otherwise
        there is no conclusion to draw.)
        
        [Nb: $AB : \rr \ra \rr$ is the function
        given by 

        $$
        (AB)(t) = A(t)B(t)
        $$

        as per the definition of function multiplication. !!]

        The first term on the right-hand side, for
        its part, comes out to

        |> ImageRight
            src=images/svg_ch4_RATS_first_term_cloud.svg

        $$
        {A(t_0 + \Delta{}t)B(t_0) - A(t_0)B(t_0) \over \Delta{}t}
        $$

        when written out, or

        $$
        B(t_0)\cdot{A(t_0 + \Delta{}t) - A(t_0) \over \Delta{}t}
        $$

        by factoring “$B(t_0)$” from the numerator; but

        $$
        {A(t_0 + \Delta{}t) - A(t_0) \over \Delta{}t}
        $$

        approaches

        $$
        A'(t_0)
        $$

        as $\Delta t$ approaches $0$, being a difference 
        quotient 
        (cf. Note >>_difference_quotient_limit_ of Exercise >>_difference_quotient_illustration_),
        so the whole term approaches

        |> Boxed

            $$
            B(t_0)A'(t_0)
            $$

        as $\Delta t$ approaches $0$, reintroducing the “$B(t_0)$”
        back in.

        Lastly the second term on the right-hand side
        comes out to

        |> ImageRight
            src=images/svg_ch4_RATS_second_term_cloud.svg

        $$
        {A(t_0 + \Delta{}t)B(t_0+\Delta{}t) - A(t_0+ \Delta{}t)B(t_0) \over \Delta{}t}
        $$

        or

        $$
        A(t_0 + \Delta{}t)\cdot{B(t_0+\Delta{}t) - B(t_0) \over \Delta{}t}
        $$

        when factoring out the common term $A(t_0 + \Delta{}t)$
        from the numerator; on the one hand,

        $$
        {B(t_0+\Delta{}t) - B(t_0) \over \Delta{}t}
        $$

        approaches

        $$
        B'(t_0)
        $$

        as $\Delta{}t$ approaches $0$ by the property of the
        difference quotient
        (cf. Note >>_difference_quotient_limit_ of Exercise >>_difference_quotient_illustration_),
        while

        $$
        A(t_0 + \Delta{}t)
        $$

        approaches

        $$
        A(t_0)
        $$

        as $\Delta t$ approaches 
        $0$ (technically: differentiability, that we are assuming,
        implies continuity, and continuity implies that $A(t_0 + \Delta{}t)$ approaches
        $A(t_0)$),
        so as the
        
        __product-of-two-things-each-approaching-a-certain-value-approaches-the-product-of-the-two-approached-values__

        the whole term approaches

        |> Boxed
            $$
            A(t_0)B'(t_0)
            $$

        as $\Delta t$ approaches $0$.

        |> SolutionNote

            We deduce 

            |> Boxed
                $$
                (AB)'(t_0) = B(t_0)A'(t_0) + A(t_0)B'(t_0)
                $$

            since, as the three terms inch closer and closer to

            $$
            (AB)'(t_0),\,\,B(t_0)A'(t_0)\,\,\,\te{ and }\,\,\,A(t_0)B'(t_0)
            $$

            respectively as $\Delta t$ approaches $0$, it
            would be impossible for the equation boxed in red
            to _not_ hold—if this equation
            didn't hold, then
            at some point, “something would have to break”
            in the original equation...

            |> Image
                src=images/svg_ch4_RATS_equation_over_Dt.svg

            ...as $\Delta t$ goes to $0$.

        |> SolutionNote
            Keeping things alphabetical everywhere, the
            same equation is more often written

            |> Boxed
                $$
                (AB)'(t_0) = A'(t_0)B(t_0) + A(t_0)B'(t_0)
                $$

            with “$A'(t_0)B(t_0)$” in the middle, valid
            when $A$ and $B$ (and therefore, one can show by
            this computation, $AB$) are differentiable at $t_0 \in \rr$,
            or 

            |> Boxed
                $$
                (AB)' = A'B + AB'
                $$

            more shortly, for $A, B : \rr \ra \rr$ differentiable, or

            |> Boxed
                $$
                (fg)' = f'g + fg'
                $$

            if we replace “$A$” and “$B$” by the more familiar “$f$” and “$g$”.
            !! for $f, g : \rr \ra \rr$ differentiable.

        |> SolutionNote
            This result is known as the _product rule_.

